<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hulagu Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1217–<a href="/years/1265">1265</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Hulagu · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borjigin">Borjigin</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hulagu Khan was a Mongol prince, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, who led the Mongol campaigns into south-western Asia in the 1250s. In 1258 his forces sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and executed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>. He founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, which ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and neighbouring lands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan.jpg" alt="Hulagu Khan" width="281" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rashid-al-Din Hamadani · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hulagu_Khan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hulagu, also written Hulegu or Hülegü, was born around 1217 to Tolui, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghaghtani-beki">Sorghaghtani Beki</a>, an influential Keraite princess. He was a brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke Khan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a>. Little is known of his childhood. His mother, a Christian of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a>, arranged for all her sons to become Mongol leaders, and Hulagu was friendly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>; his favourite wife, Doquz Khatun, and his closest friend and general, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitbuqa">Kitbuqa</a>, were also Christians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a>, installed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a> in 1251, charged Hulagu with conquering or destroying the remaining Muslim states of south-western Asia, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>. Two-tenths of the empire's fighting men were gathered for his army in 1253. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lurs">Lurs</a> were destroyed and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-assassins">Assassins</a> surrendered their fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamut">Alamut</a> in early 1256. He chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> as his base.</p><p>The army set out for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in November 1257, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> laid siege on 29 January 1258. The city surrendered on 10 February, and the Mongols entered on 13 February and began a week of destruction, including that of the Grand Library. Death counts vary widely, from a low estimate of about 90,000 to a million. In 1260 the Mongols took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, but Hulagu then withdrew most of his force and left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> over the succession after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a>'s death. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ain-jalut">Battle of Ain Jalut</a> in 1260 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutuz">Qutuz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> destroyed the Mongol force left behind.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Kublai became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a>, Hulagu returned to his lands by 1262. He was drawn into war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berke">Berke Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, a Muslim convert who had allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>, and suffered a severe defeat north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> in 1263. He sent letters to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> seeking an alliance, among them one of 10 April 1262 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a>, but never gained one. He fell ill in January 1265 and died on 8 February 1265; he was buried on Shahi Island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-urmia">Lake Urmia</a>, and his tomb has never been found.</p><p>He founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>. He employed Shams al-Din Juvayni as sahib-divan, while Ata-Malik Juvayni governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and helped repair it, and he patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-al-din-al-tusi">Nasir al-Din al-Tusi</a>, for whom the Maragheh observatory was built.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hulegu Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Hulagu Khan</b>'s forces took the city in February 1258, executed Caliph al-Musta'sim, and sacked its libraries, including the House of Wisdom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Smbat's embassy to Karakorum brought back in 1250 a guarantee of Cilicia's integrity; Het'um I visited Möngke Khan in 1253 and fought under <b>Hulagu</b> at Aleppo and Damascus in 1259–1260, where Arab historians charge his troops with a massacre and arson in the main mosque.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ilkhanate grew out of the westward expansion of the Mongol Empire under <b>Hulagu Khan</b>, a grandson of Genghis Khan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hülegü</b>'s campaign in the Middle East destroyed Baghdad in 1258.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mongol campaigns against the Khwarazmian Empire in 1219–1221 destroyed cities such as Urgench, Merv and Nishapur, and in 1258 <b>Hulagu Khan</b>’s army sacked Baghdad and put the last Abbasid caliph, al-Musta’sim, to death.</blockquote><a href="/government/nomadic-confederation">Nomadic confederation · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>After a short siege, the Abbasid Caliph al-Musta'sim surrendered Baghdad to <b>Hulagu Khan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-10">10 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maragha">Maragheh</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.39,46.24&amp;year=1248" aria-label="Maragheh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Hulagu Khan"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4074 4314h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4074 4314h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=37.39,46.24&amp;year=1248">What was here in 1248? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
